cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Optical circuit intent: when a retune is legal, what it costs, and what the plant is actually doing"
abstract: >-
  Six models for the circuit layer under a training job that crosses a hall
  boundary: an intent language that compiles to vendor-neutral operations; a
  legality ladder that says when a retune is invisible, when it stalls the job
  and when it kills it, with two defensible objectives that disagree over a
  wide band of retune times; a radix model in which free optical ports are
  stranded because they are on the wrong trunk; a checkpoint model separating
  the stop tax you can see in a training curve from the contention tax you
  cannot; a declared-versus-measured comparison with a drift forecast built on
  the published error-rate relation; a capacity-debt ledger in the shape of
  an aging schedule; and a reader for the raw files of one measured link,
  the HEDGE testbed, on which the first FEC alarm ran 88 and 39 seconds
  ahead of the outage and the outage began inside the transponder poll in
  which the last wavelength failed.
authors:
